<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, recovered kidney function (N00.9)</content>. C3 108 mg/dL is now normal, as expected within eight weeks, which supports the diagnosis and rules out the persistent low C3 of C3 glomerulopathy. Creatinine 0.92 mg/dL (eGFR 116), potassium 4.2 mmol/L, and blood pressure 116/74 off all medicines. Microscopic hematuria is down to 8 red cells per high-power field and proteinuria to 180 mg/g; both can take months to clear and need watching, but neither calls for a biopsy now. The long-term outlook for kidney function is very good. [S]<list><item>Urinalysis and urine protein-to-creatinine ratio at six months and at a year.</item><item>If proteinuria stays above 150 mg/g at six months, start an ACE inhibitor and ask nephrology about a biopsy.</item><item>Blood pressure every year, lifelong.</item><item>Avoid NSAIDs when he can; acetaminophen first for pain.</item><item>Same-day review for dark urine, swelling, or fever with a sore throat.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Standardized office blood pressure</td><td>Every visit; yearly once recovered</td><td>Completed today</td><td>116/74 mmHg off all medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Volume status: weight, edema, lungs, jugular venous pressure</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weight 61.0 kg; no edema</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine with eGFR, BUN, and potassium</td><td>At diagnosis, about weekly while creatinine is raised or on a diuretic, then at six to eight weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 0.92 mg/dL (eGFR 116), potassium 4.2 mmol/L</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine microscopy for red cells and casts, and urine protein-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>At diagnosis, at six weeks, at six months, and at a year, until hematuria and proteinuria clear</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Red cells 8 per high-power field, no casts; protein-to-creatinine ratio 180 mg/g; next at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Complement C3 (with C4 at diagnosis)</td><td>At diagnosis, at about two weeks, and at six to eight weeks until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>C3 108 mg/dL, normal; no further C3 testing needed</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Serology for other glomerular diseases (ANA, ANCA, anti-GBM) and kidney biopsy</td><td>Only if atypical: C3 low beyond eight weeks, creatinine still rising, nephrotic-range proteinuria persisting, or systemic features</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>C3 normal by six weeks and kidney function recovered</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication reconciliation: NSAID avoidance and dose review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No NSAIDs; acetaminophen during the cold</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sodium below 2 g a day, fluid limit while edematous, and daily weights</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Moderate salt; no fluid limit</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Nephrology consultation</td><td>If proteinuria persists at six months or hematuria worsens</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Results sent to Dr. Okonkwo-Brandt; no further nephrology visit unless the six-month urine is abnormal</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Swelling gone and back to his usual weight of about 61 kg within two weeks. Outcome by 1784-09-29: 61.2 kg with no edema at two weeks [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg with no blood pressure medicine needed by six weeks. Outcome by 1784-10-27: 116/74 off all medicines at six weeks; the diuretic was stopped at two weeks [S]</item><item>Creatinine back to normal by six weeks. Outcome by 1784-10-27: 0.92 mg/dL at six weeks, from a peak of 1.78 [S]</item><item>C3 back to normal within eight weeks. Outcome by 1784-10-27: 108 mg/dL at six weeks [S]</item><item>Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio below 150 mg/g. Outcome by 1784-10-27: Not yet met: 180 mg/g at six weeks, from 1400; recheck at six months [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: KDIGO 2021 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Glomerular Diseases (Kidney Int 2021;100(4S):S1-S276) (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, chronic kidney disease and hypertension monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Urine tests and a blood pressure check in six months, then at a year. Yearly preventive visit. Same-day visit for dark urine, swelling, or fever with a sore throat. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
